course: books
lesson: 43
title: The Wind in the Willows, chapter V, part 3: Dulce Domum (a dusty home, a sardine supper, and voices outside)
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Inside, the Mole sees dust everywhere, a cold, small, shabby, long-forgotten house — and flops onto a hall-chair in despair. Why did he drag poor Ratty here, when he could be toasting his toes at home? But the Rat isn't listening. He is running about lighting lamps and candles: «What a capital little house this is! So compact! So well planned!»
explain: The Rat lights a roaring fire and sets the Mole dusting. Then the Mole despairs again: there's nothing to eat, not a crumb! «What a fellow you are for giving in!» says the Rat. He has spotted a sardine-opener — so there must be sardines somewhere. They hunt and find sardines, biscuits and a sausage. The Rat jokes about the missing luxuries and comes back from the cellar with bottles of beer.
explain: The Rat asks the Mole to tell him all about his house, and the Mole forgets his sadness: this came from an aunt, that was a bargain, this other thing he saved up for by «going without». The Rat is starving, but he nods and says «wonderful» — he knows what his friend needs.
explain: Just as the Rat gets the sardine tin open, there are scuffling feet and tiny voices outside: «Now, all in a line — hold the lantern up a bit, Tommy». It's the field-mice, out carol-singing, as they do every year — and they always come to Mole End last. Eight or ten of them stand in a half-circle in red woolly scarves, and «one, two, three!» they begin.
fact: The Mole sees «the dust lying thick on everything … the cheerless, deserted look of the long-neglected house, and its narrow, meagre dimensions» (meagre = small and poor).
fact: «O Ratty! why ever did I do it? Why did I bring you to this poor, cold little place … when you might have been at River Bank … toasting your toes before a blazing fire».
fact: «The Rat paid no heed to his doleful self-reproaches» (doleful = sad; self-reproaches = blaming yourself). «What a capital little house this is! … Everything here and everything in its place!» (Capital = excellent.)
fact: The Rat admires the «little sleeping-bunks in the wall» and orders: «you get a duster, Mole … and try and smarten things up a bit. Bustle about, old chap!»
fact: Mole «promptly had another fit of the blues» (the blues = feeling very sad): «I've nothing to give you—nothing—not a crumb!»
fact: The Rat: «What a fellow you are for giving in! … I saw a sardine-opener on the kitchen dresser … and everybody knows that means there are sardines about somewhere in the neighbourhood.»
fact: They «foraged» (searched for food) and found «a tin of sardines—a box of captain's biscuits, nearly full—and a German sausage encased in silver paper». (Captain's biscuits = plain, hard biscuits, like crackers.)
fact: The Rat: «I know some animals who would give their ears to be sitting down to supper with us to-night!» The Mole: «No bread! … no butter, no——» The Rat, grinning: «No pâté de foie gras, no champagne!» (two very fancy, expensive foods.)
fact: From the cellar the Rat brings «a bottle of beer in each paw and another under each arm»: «Self-indulgent beggar you seem to be, Mole … Deny yourself nothing.» (Self-indulgent = treating yourself — said as a friendly joke.)
fact: The Mole tells how things were got: «through a windfall from an aunt» (windfall = money you didn't expect), «a wonderful find and a bargain», or «out of laborious savings and a certain amount of “going without.”»
fact: «Rat, who was desperately hungry but strove to conceal it» (tried to hide it), nods and says «“wonderful,” and “most remarkable,”» at intervals.
fact: Voices outside: «Now, all in a line—hold the lantern up a bit, Tommy—clear your throats first—no coughing after I say one, two, three.—Where's young Bill?»
fact: The Mole, «with a touch of pride»: the field-mice «go round carol-singing regularly at this time of the year … they come to Mole End last of all; and I used to give them hot drinks, and supper too sometimes, when I could afford it».
fact: «Some eight or ten little fieldmice stood in a semicircle, red worsted comforters round their throats» (a worsted comforter = a woollen scarf), «their feet jigging for warmth», lit by «a horn lantern».
fact: Talk in this lesson: «mid» = so-so, nothing special; «it's giving» = it looks like; «heaps» (Australia, NZ) = lots; «do the needful» (India) = do what has to be done; «now now» (South Africa) = in a little while.
exercise: What does the Mole see first when he lights the lamp? | Dust lying thick on everything | A table laid for supper by the field-mice | A big hole in the roof | A sleeping weasel
exercise: What does the Mole do when he sees how neglected the house is? | Collapses on a hall-chair, nose on his paws | Starts dusting and polishing at once, very cheerfully | Laughs | Runs back up the tunnel
exercise: What does the Mole blame himself for? | Bringing the Rat to a poor, cold place on a night like this | Forgetting to lock the front door when he left in spring | Losing the key | Breaking the glass ball
exercise: What is the Rat doing while the Mole moans? | Running about, opening doors, lighting lamps and candles | Sitting quietly in the hall-chair, waiting | Eating | Asleep
exercise: «What a capital little house this is!» What does «capital» mean here? | Excellent | The biggest town of a country | A big letter | Lots of money
exercise: Which part of the house does the Rat especially praise? | The sleeping-bunks in the wall | The huge banqueting-hall with its oak beams | The stables | The boat-house
exercise: What job does the Rat give the Mole? | Dusting and smartening up | Fetching water from the pond outside | Cooking | Mending the roof
exercise: Who makes the fire? | The Rat, with armfuls of wood and coal | The Mole, who knows where everything is kept | The field-mice | Nobody — the fire is already lit
exercise: What gives the Mole his next «fit of the blues»? | There's nothing in the house to eat | The fire won't light because the wood is wet | The Rat wants to go | His bed is broken
exercise: What does the Rat say the sardine-opener means? | There are sardines somewhere about | The Mole loves fish more than anything | Someone has stolen the sardines | The Mole must buy sardines
exercise: What do they find when they forage? | Sardines, captain's biscuits, a German sausage | Bread, butter, cheese and a big jar of jam | Nothing at all | Cold chicken and ham
exercise: «Captain's biscuits» are… | Plain hard biscuits, like crackers | Sweet chocolate biscuits for sea captains | Dog biscuits | Cakes
exercise: «No bread! … no butter, no——» Who says this, and how? | The Mole, groaning | The Rat, laughing | The field-mice | Nobody
exercise: «No pâté de foie gras, no champagne!» Why does the Rat say this? | To joke the Mole out of his gloom | Because he truly wants those foods and is cross | He is ordering them | He is reading a menu
exercise: What does the Rat find in the cellar? | Bottles of beer | A box of gold coins left by the Mole's aunt | A sleeping hedgehog | A sack of wet coal
exercise: «Self-indulgent beggar you seem to be, Mole.» How is the Rat speaking? | As a friendly joke | Angrily, as a real telling-off | Sadly, almost crying | Rudely, to hurt him
exercise: What does the Rat ask the Mole to tell him about? | His house and how he made it what it is | His life before he met the Rat, with every detail | The Wild Wood | His aunt's money
exercise: Which of these did the Mole NOT say about how he got his things? | Toad gave them to him | A windfall from an aunt | A bargain at a market sale | Savings and «going without»
exercise: How does the Rat behave while the Mole shows off his things? | He nods and says «wonderful», hiding his hunger | He yawns and tells the Mole to hurry up, he's starving | He eats in secret | He falls asleep
exercise: What noise comes from the forecourt just as the Rat opens the sardines? | Small feet scuffling and tiny voices | A loud knock and a gruff voice | A dog barking | Bells
exercise: «Hold the lantern up a bit, Tommy.» Who is speaking? | One of the field-mice | The Rat | The Badger | The Otter
exercise: Why are the field-mice there? | They go carol-singing every year at this time | They are lost in the snow, like the hedgehogs | They want to buy the house | The Rat sent for them
exercise: When do the field-mice always come to Mole End? | Last of all | First of all, before anyone else | Never before | At midnight sharp
exercise: What did the Mole use to give them? | Hot drinks, and supper sometimes | Money and new scarves | Nothing | Presents of cheese
exercise: How many field-mice are there? | Eight or ten | Only two of them | Exactly twelve | About twenty or so
exercise: What are they wearing round their throats? | Red woollen scarves | Golden chains | Blue ribbons | Nothing
test: Kayla: «the Rat's mid about Mole's house, says it's a dump.» (mid = so-so) Put it right. | He praises it: «a capital little house», so well planned | Right: he says it is far too small and dusty to stay in, and wants to go home now | He says it is better than Toad Hall | He says nothing about it
test: Is this TRUE or FALSE? «The Rat is not hungry, so he lets the Mole talk.» | False: he is desperately hungry but hides it | True: he had a big supper at Badger's house only an hour before | True, he never eats | False: he eats while Mole talks
test: Why does the Rat keep giving the Mole little jobs, like dusting? | Busy hands stop the Mole sinking into sadness | He wants a clean, tidy house to sleep in, because dust makes him sneeze | He is lazy | The Mole asked for jobs
test: «They come to Mole End last of all,» says Mole with pride. Why is he proud? | Ending at his home shows they value him | The last house in a village is always the biggest and the richest | They give him money | He is the oldest
test: Arjun: «the Rat just does the needful and opens the tin, innit.» (do the needful = do what's necessary) But first, how did he even know there were sardines? | He saw a sardine-opener and worked it out | The Mole told him right away where the tins were kept in the cupboard | He smelt them | He brought them
test: Looking back: in chapter III, the Rat worked something out from a small clue in the snow. What? | From the Mole's cut shin: a door-scraper, a door-mat, a door | From a footprint in the mud: that a weasel was hiding nearby | From smoke: a fire | From a feather: a bird
test: So the Rat reasoned «door-scraper → door» in chapter III and now «sardine-opener → …». What? | Sardines somewhere in the house | A little shop just round the corner | A fish pond out in the forecourt | A rowing-boat moored by the bank
test: Looking back: what was in the Rat's luncheon-basket in chapter I? | Cold chicken, tongue, ham, beef, gherkins, rolls, ginger beer… | Only bread and cheese, wrapped in paper | Sardines and biscuits | Nothing — it was empty
test: Looking back: what food did Toad show off in his caravan lockers in chapter II? | Biscuits, potted lobster, sardines | Pies, cakes and big puddings with custard | Bread and honey | Fried ham
test: Looking back: in chapter I, why had the Mole left his home so suddenly? | Spring called him up out of his cleaning | A flood had washed right into his little front hall | The Rat fetched him | He had no food
test: Looking back: in Badger's kitchen in chapter IV, what hung from the rafters? | Hams, herbs, onions and eggs | Sardine tins | Nothing | Beer bottles
test: Mia, lowkey shook (a bit upset): «Badger had heaps of food and the Mole's got nothing, that's sad.» What makes the Mole's supper feel like a feast anyway? | A friend who makes the best of it with jokes and kindness | Lots of money | The Badger arrives through a tunnel with a basket of food | The Rat's basket
test: What would happen if the Rat had agreed «yes, it's a poor cold little place»? | The Mole would feel even worse about his home | The Mole would cheer up at once and laugh about it | Nothing | They'd go to the Badger's
test: Is this TRUE or FALSE? «The field-mice come to Mole End first because it's the closest house.» | False: they come last of all | True: it is the closest, so they come first | True, and they never go anywhere else | False: they never come at all
test: ★★★ «A bottle of beer in each paw and another under each arm.» How many bottles does the Rat bring? | 4 | 2 | 3 | 6
test: ★★★ The Rat lists foods they DON'T have: «No pâté de foie gras, no champagne!» Why is this funny after the Mole's «No bread! no butter»? | The Mole names simple foods; the Rat names wildly fancy ones | The Rat has never heard of bread and butter in his life | Champagne is a kind of bread | The Rat is angry
test: ★★★ «I know some animals who would give their ears to be sitting down to supper with us.» Does he mean they'd really cut off their ears? | No — it means they'd want it very much | Yes, in those days ears were paid in shops for food | Yes, the field-mice will | No, he means their tails
exam: Identify the article whose presence led the Rat to infer the existence of sardines within the dwelling. | A sardine-opener upon the kitchen dresser | A tin of sardines, already opened and partly eaten, upon the hall-chair | A fishing rod | A picture of fish
exam: Ascertain the provisions discovered by the two animals in the course of their search. | A tin of sardines, captain's biscuits and a German sausage | A loaf, butter and pâté de foie gras, with a bottle of champagne | Nothing whatsoever | Cold chicken
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the Rat's conduct during the Mole's account of his possessions? | Concealed hunger and courteous attention | Open impatience, and repeated demands that supper be served without delay | Indifference | Sleep
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the custom of the field-mice. | Annual carol-singing, concluding at Mole End | A monthly visit to the Badger's house, by the back door | Singing at the River Bank | No such custom
